          "en": "Four families of tools test the security of an application. They do not replace each other: each\none looks at the application from a different angle.\n\n| | SAST | DAST | SCA | IAST |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Full name | Static Application Security Testing | Dynamic Application Security Testing | Software Composition Analysis | Interactive Application Security Testing |\n| What is analysed | **Your** source code | The **running** application | The **dependencies** (third-party libraries) | The running application, instrumented by an agent |\n| Needs | The Git repository | A test URL | The dependency files (`package-lock.json`, `requirements.txt`) | An agent in the runtime + functional tests |\n| Typically finds | Injections, hard-coded secrets, weak cryptography | Missing headers, misconfigurations, exploitable injections | Known CVEs in libraries | Injections actually reached during the tests |\n| Points to the faulty line | Yes | No (a URL and a parameter) | No (a package and a version) | Yes |\n| Place in the pipeline | From the commit | After a test deployment | From the commit | During integration tests |\n| False positives | Many | Few | Few (but a lot of noise) | Few |\n\nSAST is the **earliest** check (\"shift left\"): it runs on the developer's machine and in the pull\nrequest, before the application is even built. It is also the only one that points straight at the\nfile and line to fix.\n"
